---
/**
 * The home page after the hero: why someone who knows Graphviz would pick the port, and how
 * to start using it. The figures come from src/data/fidelity.json and platforms.json.
 */
import { Code, TabItem, Tabs } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import { fidelity, fmt, pct, url } from '../../lib/data';
import platforms from '../../data/platforms.json';

const e2e = fidelity.e2e;
const cc = platforms.c_linux_vs_macos;

const js = `npm install @graphviz-pure/wasm

import init, { render } from '@graphviz-pure/wasm';

await init();
const svg = render('digraph { a -> b }', 'dot', 'svg');`;
const workers = `import { initSync, render } from '@graphviz-pure/wasm';
import wasm from '@graphviz-pure/wasm/graphviz_pure_wasm_bg.wasm';

initSync({ module: wasm });
const svg = render('digraph { a -> b }', 'dot', 'svg');`;
const rust = `cargo add graphviz-pure

let svg = graphviz_pure::render_dot("digraph { a -> b }", "dot", "svg")?;`;
const cli = `brew install graphviz-pure/tap/graphviz-pure    # macOS and Linux
cargo install graphviz-pure-cli                 # anywhere Rust runs

graphviz-pure -Tsvg graph.gv -o graph.svg       # the same flags as dot`;
const windows = `scoop bucket add graphviz-pure https://github.com/graphviz-pure/scoop-bucket
scoop install graphviz-pure/graphviz-pure`;
const docker = `docker run --rm -i ghcr.io/graphviz-pure/graphviz-pure -Tsvg < graph.gv > graph.svg`;
---

<div class="home">
	<section aria-labelledby="why">
		<h2 id="why">Why not just use Graphviz?</h2>
		<p class="intro">
			Graphviz has drawn graphs since 1991, and it does it very well. It is also a C program built from system libraries and plugins, which
			makes it awkward to embed, and what it draws depends a little on the machine it runs on. graphviz-pure keeps the drawings and changes
			the rest.
		</p>
		<ol class="reasons">
			<li>
				<h3>It draws what Graphviz draws.</h3>
				<p>
					It is not a lookalike. All eight layout engines were ported from the Graphviz 16.1.0 source, function by function, quirks included.
					Given the same math functions, its SVG matches Graphviz's byte for byte on{' '}
					{e2e.identical === e2e.total ? 'all ' : ''}<strong class="num">{fmt(e2e.identical)}</strong>{e2e.identical === e2e.total ? '' : ` of ${fmt(e2e.total)}`} graphs in the test suite.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<h3>It is one self-contained package.</h3>
				<p>
					A single binary for the command line, a Rust crate with no C inside, or one npm package for browsers, Node.js, Deno, Bun and
					Cloudflare Workers. It needs no system libraries, plugins or C compiler, and it has no <code>unsafe</code> code.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<h3>It gives the same picture on every machine.</h3>
				<p>
					graphviz-pure writes byte-for-byte the same output on Linux, Windows, Macs and in every browser, for <strong class="num">100%</strong>
					of the test graphs. In the historical cross-platform comparison, C Graphviz managed <strong class="num">{pct(cc.agree, cc.total)}</strong> between Linux and a
					Mac. graphviz-pure does all its math in pure Rust, with the same portable math library on every platform, and CI compares every output
					byte for byte across them. No build borrows the operating system's math library, on any machine.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="get-started">
		<h2 id="get-started">Get started</h2>
		<div class="start">
			<div class="tabs">
				<Tabs syncKey="install">
					<TabItem label="JavaScript">
						<Code code={js} lang="js" />
						<p class="note">
							Also on JSR for Deno. On Cloudflare Workers, import the <code>.wasm</code> file yourself:
						</p>
						<Code code={workers} lang="js" />
					</TabItem>
					<TabItem label="Rust">
						<Code code={rust} lang="rust" />
						<p class="note">
							<code>render_dot</code> takes the DOT source, an engine and an output format. The <a href={url('api/')}>API docs</a> cover the
							rest.
						</p>
					</TabItem>
					<TabItem label="Command line">
						<Code code={cli} lang="sh" />
						<p class="note">On Windows, use Scoop:</p>
						<Code code={windows} lang="powershell" />
						<p class="note">Or run the container image:</p>
						<Code code={docker} lang="sh" />
					</TabItem>
				</Tabs>
			</div>
			<ul class="facts">
				<li><strong>Every engine:</strong> <code>dot</code>, <code>neato</code>, <code>fdp</code>, <code>sfdp</code>, <code>twopi</code>, <code>circo</code>, <code>osage</code> and <code>patchwork</code>.</li>
				<li><strong>Every common format:</strong> SVG, JSON, DOT, xdot and plain text everywhere; PNG and JPEG from the command line.</li>
				<li><strong>A drop-in <code>dot</code>:</strong> the <code>graphviz-pure</code> command takes the same flags as <code>dot</code> and exits with the same status.</li>
				<li><strong>Often faster:</strong> the historical <a href="https://github.com/graphviz-pure/graphviz-pure/blob/master/README.md#performance">dot benchmarks</a> measured orthogonal edges on Graphviz's large <code>b100</code> graph at 20 seconds instead of over five minutes.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>
</div>

<style>
	.intro {
		max-width: 44rem;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.reasons {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 2rem;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
		counter-reset: reason;
	}
	.reasons li {
		margin: 0;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		counter-increment: reason;
	}
	.reasons h3 {
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-lg);
		font-weight: 650;
		font-stretch: 112%;
		line-height: 1.3;
		color: var(--key);
	}
	.reasons h3::before {
		content: counter(reason);
		display: block;
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
		font-family: var(--face-mono);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xs);
		font-weight: 400;
		color: var(--register);
	}
	.reasons p {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.reasons strong {
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
	}
	.start {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
		gap: 2.5rem;
		align-items: start;
	}
	.tabs :global(.expressive-code) {
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
	}
	.note {
		margin: 1rem 0 0;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--key-3);
	}
	.facts {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
	}
	.facts li {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0.75rem 0;
		border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.facts strong {
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
	}
	@media (max-width: 50rem) {
		.reasons,
		.start {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
